A complete one-pot Sunday feast. A tender pork loin roast is floured and hard-seared for a flavorful browned crust, then gently braised alongside sweet carrots, celery, onions, and russet potatoes in a savory broth laced with sweet apple cider and fresh herbs. The strained braising liquid transforms into a silky, cider-scented homemade pan gravy.

*Adapted from [The Seasoned Mom](https://www.theseasonedmom.com/dutch-oven-pork-roast/).*

## Ingredients

### Pork & Braised Vegetables
- ⅓ cup all-purpose flour (for dredging)
- Kosher salt and freshly cracked black pepper, to taste
- 4 lb boneless pork loin roast
- 1 tablespoon unsalted butter
- 2 tablespoons extra-virgin olive oil
- 2 cups low-sodium chicken broth, divided
- 5 large carrots, peeled and sliced into 2-inch chunks
- 2 medium yellow onions, thinly sliced
- 4 large celery ribs, washed and cut into ½-inch crescents
- 2 cloves garlic, minced
- 1 cup apple cider (or unfiltered apple juice)
- 4 sprigs fresh thyme (or ¼ teaspoon dried thyme)
- 4 sprigs fresh rosemary (or ¼ teaspoon dried rosemary)
- 2 dried bay leaves
- 3 medium russet potatoes, peeled and cut into eighths

### Homemade Cider Pan Gravy
- 2 tablespoons unsalted butter
- 2 tablespoons all-purpose flour
- 1 to 1 ½ cups strained pot drippings and braising juices
- Additional kosher salt and black pepper, to taste

## Instructions

1. **Preheat and Dredge:** Position your oven rack in the lower-middle slot and preheat to 350°F (175°C). Spread ⅓ cup of all-purpose flour on a sheet of parchment or wax paper and season heavily with salt and black pepper. Pat the pork roast dry with paper towels, then roll and press it into the flour until coated on every side.
2. **Brown the Pork:** Set a large (6-to-7-quart) enameled cast iron Dutch oven over medium-high heat. Add 1 tablespoon butter and 2 tablespoons olive oil. Once hot and shimmering, place the dredged pork into the pot. Sear without moving for 4 to 6 minutes per side until each side forms a rich golden-brown crust. Transfer the seared pork onto a platter.
3. **Deglaze and Soften Aromatics:** Reduce the stovetop heat to medium. Pour in 1 cup of the chicken broth, immediately using a wooden spoon to vigorously scrape up all the flavorful caramelized fond stuck to the bottom of the Dutch oven. Stir in the sliced carrots, onions, celery, and minced garlic. Cook for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the onions become translucent and tender.
4. **First Stage Braise:** Lay the seared pork roast directly on top of the softened vegetables. Pour in the remaining 1 cup chicken broth and the apple cider. Tuck the thyme sprigs, rosemary, and bay leaves into the liquid around the meat. Sprinkle a pinch of salt and pepper over the surface. Cover tightly with the heavy lid and transfer to the oven to bake for 35 to 40 minutes.
5. **Add the Potatoes:** Carefully remove the hot Dutch oven from the oven and lift the lid. Arrange the cut potato chunks into the bubbling liquid around the edges of the roast. Cover again and return to the oven for 40 to 45 minutes, or until the potatoes pierce easily with a fork and a meat thermometer inserted into the center of the pork reads 145°F (63°C).
6. **Rest the Roast:** Transfer the pork loin to a cutting board, tent loosely with foil, and let rest for 10 to 15 minutes before carving so the juices redistribute. Keep the vegetables warm in the covered pot.
7. **Whip up the Cider Pan Gravy:** Ladle or pour the liquid from the Dutch oven through a fine-mesh strainer into a heat-safe measuring cup, yielding about 1 to 1 ½ cups of concentrated juices. In a separate saucepan over medium heat, melt 2 tablespoons of butter until bubbly. Whisk in 2 tablespoons of flour and cook for 30 seconds to cook out raw flour flavor. Slowly pour in the warm strained drippings in a steady stream while whisking continuously to prevent lumps. Simmer for 1 to 2 minutes until thickened to a velvety gravy. Taste and season with additional salt and pepper as needed.
8. **Slice and Serve:** Carve the rested pork into ½-inch slices and serve surrounded by the braised carrots and potatoes, drizzling generous ladlefuls of cider gravy over everything.

## Notes

- **Cut of Meat:** Pork loin roast is lean and tender, best cooked to medium (145°F / 63°C with a 3-minute rest). If using boneless pork shoulder (Boston butt), extend the braising time by 45 to 60 minutes until the meat is fork-shreddable (around 195°F–200°F).
- **Herb Bundle:** Tying the fresh thyme and rosemary sprigs together with kitchen twine makes them easy to fish out before serving.
